-- title:
--   Definitions for Metaplectic formulation of the Wigner transform and applications
-- statement:
--   The objects the formalized statements of *Metaplectic formulation of the Wigner transform and applications* (arXiv:1401.3388) are stated in. Each was checked against the paper by a blind read-back and a moderator.
--
--   - `Configuration`: PDF p.3, Notation 1: real Cartesian n-space with its usual topology and Lebesgue measure. The finite product norm is equivalent to the Euclidean norm.
--   - `DoublePhaseSpace`: PDF p.3, Notation 1: coordinates 0,1,2,3 are x,p,xi_x,xi_p.
--   - `coordinateDot`: PDF p.3, Notation 1: the Euclidean dot product, written in Cartesian coordinates.
--   - `classicalHamiltonian`: PDF p.4, Theorem 4: the classical quadratic Hamiltonian (6).
--   - `hamiltonianMatrix`: PDF p.4, Hamilton equations in the proof of Theorem 4. This is the generator, in the paper's (x,p,xi_x,xi_p) order.
--   - `hamiltonianFlow`: PDF pp.4–5: the Hamiltonian flow generated by (6). A finite real matrix exponential constructs the unique linear-system flow; no period, ellipse equation, or special-time formula is built into it.
--   - `ellipticEnergy`: PDF p.5, (10): the conserved quadratic form of one coordinate pair, summed over all n coordinates. It is 2|u|^2+|v|^2-u dot v.
--   - `ellipticCoordinates`: PDF p.5, Remark 5: coordinates that turn the pair's ellipses into circles. Their squared radius is 4(2u^2+v^2-uv).
--   - `clockwiseRotation`: PDF p.5, Remark 5: clockwise rotation in the ellipse-adapted coordinates.
--   - `PhaseFunction`: PDF p.3, Notation 3: phase-space functions; statement domains below are Schwartz.
--   - `partialFourierFormula`: PDF p.3, Notation 3: the paper-normalized partial Fourier integral. Use sign=-1 for the forward transform and sign=1 for its inverse. Only Schwartz inputs and their Schwartz-derived expressions occur in targets.
--   - `phaseSliceFlow`: PDF p.5, (11): the (x,xi_p) part of the Hamiltonian flow. The other pair can be set to zero because the Hamilton equations decouple.
--   - `evolutionFormula`: PDF pp.5–6, (11)–(12): the explicit Fourier-pullback formula on Schwartz inputs. This is the formula used in (46); it does not define an unbounded exponential.
--   - `specialAngle`: PDF p.4, Theorem 4: the distinguished angle, also used in (46) on p.16.
--   - `positionCoordinate`: PDF pp.2,16, (5): multiplication by the j-th x coordinate.
--   - `momentumCoordinate`: PDF pp.2,16, (5): -i times the j-th x derivative. All asserted operator identities have the common Schwartz domain.
--   - `boppPosition`: PDF p.16, (46): the Bopp position operator computed by conjugation with S. The claimed Bopp differential formula is not used in this definition.
--   - `boppMomentum`: PDF p.16, (46): the Bopp momentum operator computed by conjugation with S.
-- source:
--   Metaplectic formulation of the Wigner transform and applications, arXiv:1401.3388v1, https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.3388v1

import Mathlib

/-!
Paper-local objects and statement targets from arXiv:1401.3388v1.
Coordinates on the double phase space are ordered (x,p,xi_x,xi_p).
The classical flow is the matrix exponential of the Hamilton equations,
not the special-time map or the claimed periodicity.

The Fourier integral helpers below are used only on Schwartz functions and
on their Schwartz Fourier transforms, linear pullbacks, polynomial multiples,
and derivatives. They make no claim to define Fourier transforms on arbitrary
L2 functions or to construct a self-adjoint generator. The Bopp coordinate
operators use the explicit formula (12), independently of their claimed
coordinate actions. All deferred analytic results are recorded in HARD.md.
Compilation is pending the separate mechanical stage.
-/

noncomputable section

open MeasureTheory
open scoped BigOperators Matrix

namespace QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Claimed50.P10

/-- PDF p.3, Notation 1: real Cartesian n-space with its usual topology and Lebesgue measure.
The finite product norm is equivalent to the Euclidean norm. -/
abbrev Configuration (n : ℕ) := Fin n → ℝ

/-- PDF p.3, Notation 1: coordinates 0,1,2,3 are x,p,xi_x,xi_p. -/
abbrev DoublePhaseSpace (n : ℕ) := Fin 4 → Configuration n

/-- PDF p.3, Notation 1: the Euclidean dot product, written in Cartesian coordinates. -/
def coordinateDot {n : ℕ} (u v : Configuration n) : ℝ := ∑ j, u j * v j

example {n : ℕ} (u : Configuration n) : coordinateDot u 0 = 0 := by
  simp [coordinateDot]

/-- PDF p.4, Theorem 4: the classical quadratic Hamiltonian (6). -/
def classicalHamiltonian {n : ℕ} (z : DoublePhaseSpace n) : ℝ :=
  2 * coordinateDot (z 2) (z 3) - coordinateDot (z 0) (z 2) -
    coordinateDot (z 1) (z 3) + 4 * coordinateDot (z 0) (z 1)

example {n : ℕ} : classicalHamiltonian (0 : DoublePhaseSpace n) = 0 := by
  simp [classicalHamiltonian, coordinateDot]

/-- PDF p.4, Hamilton equations in the proof of Theorem 4.
This is the generator, in the paper's (x,p,xi_x,xi_p) order. -/
def hamiltonianMatrix : Matrix (Fin 4) (Fin 4) ℝ :=
  !![-1, 0, 0, 2; 0, -1, 2, 0; 0, -4, 1, 0; -4, 0, 0, 1]

example : hamiltonianMatrix 0 3 = 2 := by
  rfl

/-- PDF pp.4–5: the Hamiltonian flow generated by (6).
A finite real matrix exponential constructs the unique linear-system flow;
no period, ellipse equation, or special-time formula is built into it. -/
def hamiltonianFlow {n : ℕ} (theta : ℝ) (z : DoublePhaseSpace n) :
    DoublePhaseSpace n :=
  fun a j => (NormedSpace.exp (theta • hamiltonianMatrix)).mulVec
    (fun b => z b j) a

example {n : ℕ} (z : DoublePhaseSpace n) : hamiltonianFlow 0 z = z := by
  funext a j
  simp [hamiltonianFlow]

/-- PDF p.5, (10): the conserved quadratic form of one coordinate pair,
summed over all n coordinates. It is 2|u|^2+|v|^2-u dot v. -/
def ellipticEnergy {n : ℕ} (u v : Configuration n) : ℝ :=
  ∑ j, (2 * u j ^ 2 + v j ^ 2 - u j * v j)

example {n : ℕ} : ellipticEnergy (0 : Configuration n) 0 = 0 := by
  simp [ellipticEnergy]

/-- PDF p.5, Remark 5: coordinates that turn the pair's ellipses into circles.
Their squared radius is 4(2u^2+v^2-uv). -/
def ellipticCoordinates (u v : ℝ) : Fin 2 → ℝ :=
  ![Real.sqrt 7 * u, 2 * v - u]

example : ellipticCoordinates 0 0 = 0 := by
  ext j
  fin_cases j <;> simp [ellipticCoordinates]

/-- PDF p.5, Remark 5: clockwise rotation in the ellipse-adapted coordinates. -/
def clockwiseRotation (angle : ℝ) (w : Fin 2 → ℝ) : Fin 2 → ℝ :=
  ![w 0 * Real.cos angle + w 1 * Real.sin angle,
    w 1 * Real.cos angle - w 0 * Real.sin angle]

example (w : Fin 2 → ℝ) : clockwiseRotation 0 w = w := by
  ext j
  fin_cases j <;> simp [clockwiseRotation]

/-- PDF p.3, Notation 3: phase-space functions; statement domains below are Schwartz. -/
abbrev PhaseFunction (n : ℕ) := (Configuration n × Configuration n) → ℂ

/-- PDF p.3, Notation 3: the paper-normalized partial Fourier integral.
Use sign=-1 for the forward transform and sign=1 for its inverse.
Only Schwartz inputs and their Schwartz-derived expressions occur in targets. -/
def partialFourierFormula {n : ℕ} (sign : ℝ) (f : PhaseFunction n) : PhaseFunction n :=
  fun z => ((Real.rpow (2 * Real.pi) (-(n : ℝ) / 2) : ℝ) : ℂ) *
    ∫ y : Configuration n,
      Complex.exp (Complex.I * ((sign * coordinateDot z.2 y : ℝ) : ℂ)) * f (z.1, y)

example {n : ℕ} (sign : ℝ) :
    partialFourierFormula sign (0 : PhaseFunction n) = 0 := by
  funext z
  simp [partialFourierFormula]

/-- PDF p.5, (11): the (x,xi_p) part of the Hamiltonian flow.
The other pair can be set to zero because the Hamilton equations decouple. -/
def phaseSliceFlow {n : ℕ} (theta : ℝ) (z : Configuration n × Configuration n) :
    Configuration n × Configuration n :=
  let w : DoublePhaseSpace n := ![z.1, 0, 0, z.2]
  let v := hamiltonianFlow theta w
  (v 0, v 3)

example {n : ℕ} (z : Configuration n × Configuration n) : phaseSliceFlow 0 z = z := by
  have hzero (w : DoublePhaseSpace n) : hamiltonianFlow 0 w = w := by
    funext a j
    simp [hamiltonianFlow]
  exact congrArg (fun w : DoublePhaseSpace n => (w 0, w 3))
    (hzero ![z.1, 0, 0, z.2])

/-- PDF pp.5–6, (11)–(12): the explicit Fourier-pullback formula on Schwartz inputs.
This is the formula used in (46); it does not define an unbounded exponential. -/
def evolutionFormula {n : ℕ} (theta : ℝ) (f : PhaseFunction n) : PhaseFunction n :=
  partialFourierFormula 1
    (fun z => partialFourierFormula (-1) f (phaseSliceFlow (-theta) z))

example {n : ℕ} (theta : ℝ) : evolutionFormula theta (0 : PhaseFunction n) = 0 := by
  funext z
  simp [evolutionFormula, partialFourierFormula]

/-- PDF p.4, Theorem 4: the distinguished angle, also used in (46) on p.16. -/
def specialAngle : ℝ := Real.sqrt 7 / 7 * Real.arccos (3 / 4)

example : specialAngle = Real.sqrt 7 / 7 * Real.arccos (3 / 4) := by
  rfl

/-- PDF pp.2,16, (5): multiplication by the j-th x coordinate. -/
def positionCoordinate {n : ℕ} (j : Fin n) (f : PhaseFunction n) : PhaseFunction n :=
  fun z => (z.1 j : ℂ) * f z

example {n : ℕ} (j : Fin n) : positionCoordinate j (0 : PhaseFunction n) = 0 := by
  funext z
  simp [positionCoordinate]

/-- PDF pp.2,16, (5): -i times the j-th x derivative.
All asserted operator identities have the common Schwartz domain. -/
def momentumCoordinate {n : ℕ} (j : Fin n) (f : PhaseFunction n) : PhaseFunction n :=
  fun z => -Complex.I * (fderiv ℝ f z) (Pi.single j 1, 0)

example {n : ℕ} (j : Fin n) : momentumCoordinate j (0 : PhaseFunction n) = 0 := by
  funext z
  simp [momentumCoordinate]

/-- PDF p.16, (46): the Bopp position operator computed by conjugation with S.
The claimed Bopp differential formula is not used in this definition. -/
def boppPosition {n : ℕ} (j : Fin n) (f : PhaseFunction n) : PhaseFunction n :=
  evolutionFormula specialAngle
    (positionCoordinate j (evolutionFormula (-specialAngle) f))

example {n : ℕ} (j : Fin n) : boppPosition j (0 : PhaseFunction n) = 0 := by
  funext z
  simp [boppPosition, positionCoordinate, evolutionFormula, partialFourierFormula]

/-- PDF p.16, (46): the Bopp momentum operator computed by conjugation with S. -/
def boppMomentum {n : ℕ} (j : Fin n) (f : PhaseFunction n) : PhaseFunction n :=
  evolutionFormula specialAngle
    (momentumCoordinate j (evolutionFormula (-specialAngle) f))

example {n : ℕ} (j : Fin n) : boppMomentum j (0 : PhaseFunction n) = 0 := by
  have hevolution (theta : ℝ) : evolutionFormula theta (0 : PhaseFunction n) = 0 := by
    funext z
    simp [evolutionFormula, partialFourierFormula]
  have hmomentum : momentumCoordinate j (0 : PhaseFunction n) = 0 := by
    funext z
    simp [momentumCoordinate]
  rw [boppMomentum, hevolution, hmomentum, hevolution]

end QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Claimed50.P10


